A||N||1||FieroJoe||Russel's Speed Bleeders??||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||05-16-2001||09:49 PM||||Looking through a Camaro restoration catalog (National Parts Depot), and find these new bleeder screws that make bleeding your brakes a little easier.  They allow you to do it yourself without having to mess with vacuum pumps.  It looks like a normal bleeder, and all you have to do is hook a hose up to it, unscrew it a 1/4 turn, and then just pump your brakes until you are done.  There is an internal check ball in there that will prevent air from being sucked back in.  Then when you are done, you tighten it up and then you are done.<P>These seem pretty neat, and I think I'd like to try a set.  They are $9.95 a pair, so I would need two pairs, correct? (stock 87 brakes).<P>They also come in different thread pitches. 3/8"-24 x 1", 5/16"-24 x 1", or 8mm - 1.25 x 24mm.  I assume Fiero calipers are probably the metric ones, but can anyone verify this?  I dont have any extra bleeder screws laying around to check with a pitch gauge.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||11:59 PM||||sounds neat. One of the advantages of using a vacuum pump or a pressure system is that when you are pumping up the brakes with the pedal, air bubbles can float back up the lines and be harder to get out.<P>  with the vacuum pump you can draw quite a bit of fluid out in one consistant flow - also if you spring for one you can use it on any car, and its also good for testing for vacuum leaks and testing vacuum valves.<P>  they arent that expensive - I just got one for about $30 from PartsPlus. The one short coming is, if you have new bleeder valves you need to put teflon tape on the threads, or it sucks a lot of air through the threads then you open the valve.<P>   Older bleeder valves have enough dust and dirt in the threads that this is not a problem.<P>   Ive been working on cars for about 25 years now. Wish I had bought this pump 25 years ago.||||reg||1
